{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind Journey Through Japan**\n\nTokyo’s neon glow greeted me at Narita Airport, a stark contrast to the crisp autumn air. My first stop: Shinjuku. Towering skyscrapers gave way to Golden Gai’s tiny bars, where dim lanterns and saké warmed the chilly evening. A bowl of rich ramen at Ichiran—spicy miso, tender pork—felt like a homecoming.\n\nKyoto was serene by comparison. The Kiyomizu-dera temple’s wooden terrace overlooked cherry trees already dusted with frost. A matcha latte at Maccha House, paired with a sweet mochi, tasted like tranquility. The narrow streets of Gion, lined with lanterns, whispered secrets of geisha culture.\n\nIn Osaka, the energy shifted. Dotonbori’s vibrant signs advertised takoyaki and okonomiyaki, sizzling in street-side stalls. The Osaka Castle, resplendent in golden light, stood proud over the city. A quick train ride brought me to Nara, where friendly deer roamed freely, bowing for crumbs of crackers.\n\nMy last stop: Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was solemn, a reminder of history’s weight. Yet, the city thrived—colorful flags, lively markets, a resilient spirit. A simple bowl of oysters by the harbor felt like closure.\n\nTokyo’s final night was a blur of izakayas and last-minute souvenir shopping. The Shinkansen back to Narita blurred past rice fields, mountains, and briefly glimpsed temples—each moment a postcard in motion.\n\nJapan is a paradox: ancient and modern, chaotic and peaceful, loud and silent. In just a week, it stole a piece of my heart.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06550939381122589}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.27843376994132996}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.037113383412361145}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.018043426796793938}, {"token": "wind", "logprob": -4.7801782784517854e-05}, {"token": " Journey", "logprob": -1.7555687427520752}, {"token": " Through", "l